
✅ Semantic Markup — Notes
---------------------------

1) What is Semantic Markup?
Semantic markup means using HTML elements that clearly describe their meaning and purpose.
These elements tell both the browser and the developer what the content represents, not just how it looks.
Examples:
<header>, <nav>, <main>, <section>, <article>, <footer>

2) Why Semantic Elements Were Introduced?

a) To give meaning to the layout
Old HTML used <div> everywhere. Semantic elements add meaning:
<header> → top section
<nav> → navigation links
<main> → main content
<footer> → bottom section

b) To improve accessibility
Screen readers can understand the structure better.
They can announce:
“Navigation region”, “Main content”, “Footer section”.

c) To improve SEO (Search Engine Optimization)
Search engines understand your content better when you use semantic tags.
This helps with ranking and indexing.

d) To reduce div soup
Before semantic HTML, developers used <div> for everything:
<div id="header"></div>
<div id="content"></div>
<div id="footer"></div>

This caused clutter and confusion.
Semantic elements make HTML cleaner and more readable.

e) To standardize webpage structure
Semantic elements provide a consistent structure that all developers follow.
This makes code easier to understand and maintain.

3) Examples of Semantic Elements


<header> → introduction, logo, navigation


<nav> → menu links


<main> → main content


<section> → grouped content


<article> → independent content (blog posts, news, cards)


<aside> → sidebar content


<footer> → ending section of the page



4) Simple Example
<header>
    <h1>My Website</h1>
</header>

<nav>
    <a href="#">Home</a>
    <a href="#">Contact</a>
</nav>

<main>
    <article>
        <h2>Blog Post</h2>
        <p>This is my article section.</p>
    </article>
</main>

<footer>
    <p>© 2025 MySite</p>
</footer>

